Emacsvox News -- History Of User-Visible Changes

Maintained by Emacsvox Contributors.

* Emacsvox Unreleased --- Current User-Visible Changes

** Release target: Omnivox 1.12

This development series targets Omnivox 1.12 for the next Emacsvox release.
The current guided installers still pin 1.10.0; the newer voice-management
features below need a compatible development build until the release pins
are updated. Upgrade an existing Omnivox installation separately: updating
Emacsvox does not replace your selected server or install optional engines.

** Unified voice palettes and upgrade notes

A named voice now keeps its adjustments, ordered physical choices and
individual fallback tuning together in its palette. Inheritance takes the
nearest complete entry, with the read-only acss-default palette as the root.
Copy Palette makes an independent copy; Reset restores an inherited voice,
while Delete removes a custom voice with no inherited replacement. Source
and Where Used help identify affected voices and known references.

Use the common voice editor to change named voices. The former shared
per-voice routing, migration and preset screens have been removed. Activation
selects a palette for this session; Use at Startup saves its selection in a
Presentation Profile.

Older personal palettes need attention before upgrading. Only palette schema
3 is supported, and opening an older palette does not convert it. Keep a
backup and arrange a reviewed conversion of existing palettes and their
physical choices before using this version. See /Choose And Manage Voice
Palettes/ in the manual for the current model.

** Delayed phonetic character descriptions

Character navigation now speaks a letter normally, then its phonetic name
after a pause. The names match C-e c.
Customize emacsvox-delayed-phonetic-delay to change the default
one-second pause. Toggle emacsvox-delayed-phonetic-mode to turn this feature
off or on; it starts enabled. Further input or speech cancels the pending
description. Typing and deletion do not trigger descriptions.

** Additional RHVoice voices

Get more voices now offers Alan, BDL, CLB and Ksp for RHVoice with a compatible
updated Omnivox build and separately installed RHVoice runtime. Downloads include
the required English data, start disabled and use the existing Enable and Apply
workflow. Existing external voices, including SLT, are preserved. Downloaded
RHVoice packages can be uninstalled with C-c u after disabling and applying.

** Managed voice uninstallation

The Omnivox voice browser offers C-c u to review removal of downloaded Piper,
Flite and MBROLA packages. Disable and Apply first. Reviews include all shared
speakers, known palette references and files retained for other speech sessions.
Built-in voices, imported files, engine runtimes and saved choices are preserved.
C-c U resumes incomplete cleanup.

** Faster navigation in Browse voices

Single-voice languages appear directly. Regional voices form a tree, such as
English, United Kingdom, then West Midlands. Quitting a dedicated voice list
now unwinds its window so repeated q returns out of the browser without a loop.

Omnivox voice lists offer 1 All voices, 2 Downloaded, 3 Enabled, and 4 Needs
Apply. Q lists their counts, including voices in collapsed languages.
Slash finds a voice by name and opens its language group, switching to All
voices when the quick filter hides the result. Each filter remembers its
selected row and column. Unchecked worker status remains separate from
confirmed selection changes needing Apply.

** Expanded Piper voice catalogue

Piper downloads are grouped by language, voice and quality. Slash searches
across the collection; q returns to the preceding group. Model details can
show the speakers sharing one download, with separate enablement after
installation. Single-quality voices appear directly in their language group.
The catalogue now supports e to enable or disable an installed speaker and
a to review Apply, with actions near the top of model details.
Downloads are checked and validated before they are added to the voice library.

** Bundled eSpeak variants

eSpeak engine details offer the variants reported by the running speech worker,
sorted alphabetically. Choose a base voice with b, then press P or RET to hear
the selected combination. Variants need no enabling, settings save, download
or speech restart.

Press u for /Use in palette/ to add the combination as a named voice's preferred
choice, retaining its fallbacks and tuning. The voice editor previews and saves
the change; editing a built-in palette creates a personal copy. Older workers
report missing on-demand variant support explicitly.

** Speech engine details

Aural Home's /Speech engines/ shows discovered availability and voice counts.
RET opens current main and notification discovery, timestamps and runtime
diagnostics. Old inventory is labelled as previous evidence. Opening or
refreshing these details does not run the release installer.

On WSL, /Manage installed release/ opens a separate view identifying the release
and location affected by installation, removal and /Check release engine/.
That release may differ from the active development build or remote server.
Managed checks run asynchronously and retain their diagnostics without opening
an output buffer or speaking the full result. Release management keeps current
speech running and does not select a different server.

/Browse voices/ opens an engine-specific voice browser without disturbing
the general Voice Workbench. P speaks an exact sample; t opens the existing
voice editor, where an experiment can be saved to an explicit palette
destination. q returns to engine details. Inventory updates preserve focus and
selection, and browsing never starts a sample automatically.

The bundled local launcher's engine details now provide file settings for
Piper, Flite, Eloquence and DECtalk. Flite files have individual /Load file/
switches; disabled files remain on disk and are omitted after speech restarts.
Existing environment overrides retain precedence. A bounded, silent configured
check retains the target and diagnostics without replacing live speech;
incomplete Flite discovery is reported even when built-in SLT still works.
Restarting local speech is an explicit action. Downloaded voice enablement uses
Apply to check and replace both speech streams, restoring the previous
configuration if a replacement fails. Downloads start disabled, and changing
Enabled does not change the active voices until Apply. Managed voice-library
operations currently use the bundled WSL-to-Windows launcher; remote and
direct native launches retain their own configuration paths.

** Independent output volumes

Aural Home's /Audio output/ combines notification device selection with five
Omnivox volume controls in Customize:
notification speech, tones and earcons, plus main-stream tones and earcons.
Set each from 0 to 100 percent; 100 preserves its normal level and 0 silences
that component. Changes affect subsequent audio, and saved settings survive
restarts. Independent notification levels require a separate notification
process; notifications using main output share that process's levels.

** Agent Shell response navigation

Upward movement now reaches indented response text that could previously be
skipped with only a blank-line earcon. First response rows are spoken in both
directions, including wrapped text, without leaking hidden prompt markers.

** Simpler feedback controls

Aural Home's /Understand and change feedback/ group now has three entries:
/Feedback for this item/, /Recent feedback/, and /Return to source item/.
Feedback Details combines explanation, playback, and access to changes.
Existing direct shortcuts and searchable action names remain available.
Semantic vocabulary is available through Help, search, and matching criteria.

Feedback Details offers a compact voice choice for the selected field and
access to exact sound overrides, including restoration. Individual voice spans
expand separately, simple field headings are shorter, and unsaved field changes
are marked in the report. Original and simulated playback remain clearly
labelled; previews and retained drafts do not change the captured presentation.

** Names for occasional emoji enabled by default

Occasional emoji are named before engine selection by default.
For example, =Forecast 🔮= becomes =Forecast crystal ball=
for speech, which can avoid an unnecessary engine fallback. The source text
stays unchanged. Naming defaults to at most 64 eligible occurrences per item
and supports buffer-local settings and custom names. Set
=emacsvox-emoji-naming-enabled= to nil to disable it globally or in a buffer.
The higher limit handles decorated mail subjects, including a thinking face
followed by three pizza symbols, without skipping all naming at the third emoji.

Names come from Emacs's full bundled emoji table, including =🌱= (seedling),
flags and keycaps. Arrow and harpoon symbols use bundled emoji names or their
Unicode names, covering all directions, diagonal, double and curved arrows,
including text and emoji presentation selectors.
No per-emoji approval is needed; an explicit restriction
list remains optional. Unknown sequences and other languages
keep their existing fallback behaviour. Speech and voice previews share the
policy, and Feedback Details records substitutions. Original replay preserves
the prepared text after settings change. See /Emoji Speech Names/ in the manual.

** Optional fallback tuning
Choose Customize this voice from an editor fallback row to set individual
adjustments, use the shared value, or restore the adapter default. Automatic
samples audition that row; the same voice draft and Save action own all changes.
Experiment keeps default to the chosen row's voice and adjustments. Shared
changes remain explicit; unrelated draft edits and save recovery are preserved.
Details explains the shared, actual-fallback and contextual sources of the last
confirmed sample's settings.


** Tracked speech handles early playback replies

Tracked reading now retains playback completion received while a speech request
is still being written. Stopping speech cancels pending callbacks, and failed
submissions cannot restore cancelled tracking or replay the request. Speech
started by playback callbacks gets its own submission.

Stop also cancels speech still being prepared, including a request interrupted
during its automatic stop of older speech. A newer urgent announcement can
supersede that request. Interrupted preparation releases its tracking slots.

** Voice editor gives spoken adjustment feedback

Automatic samples now start with the new adjustment value. Numeric controls
stop at their limits and announce minimum or maximum, and the automatic-sample
toggle announces on or off. Arrow and Tab navigation stop at the first and
last fields without wrapping around the editor.

Complete voice comparisons and individual auditions now share cancellation
handling. Stop and newer previews retire the previous sample's pending work;
late replies cannot start another comparison entry. Individual auditions also
have a playback timeout. A failed or interrupted write is never replayed.

With Omnivox per-choice tuning support, editor previews now preserve individual
fallback settings and contextual overrides. A row can be auditioned or compared
with its original after reordering. Playback feedback distinguishes the sample
from spoken comparison labels and audio that was accepted but never started.
Text changes, undo, save and context recapture retire stale previews. Earlier
playback evidence cannot replace a newer view's result.

** Voice palette selections apply consistently

Search and incremental-search hits now use the active palette's bolden voice.
Nonincremental searches, including regexp and history-repeat commands, speak
the destination line and cue a successful hit once.

Completion, status speech, spelling feedback, widgets, and other integrations
now retain named voices instead of freezing legacy ACSS presets. Calendar
marked dates and CPerl/table indentation use the normal speech path, and the
named-voice sampler follows the active palette. Voice Lock and explicit aural
rules retain their existing control over formatting voices.

Restart Emacs after updating to pick up the new voice defaults. Explicit
personal voice assignments are preserved; use names such as ='voice-bolden=
when those assignments should follow the palette. Legacy =voice-*-settings=
remain the fallback for voices without custom palette definitions.

** Responsive shell completion under WSL

Shell command-name completion now skips Windows drive directories by default,
avoiding long pauses scanning directories such as System32. Windows programs
still run by name, and explicit Windows filename completion remains available.
Enable =emacsvox-shell-complete-windows-commands= to restore those command-name
candidates. Native Windows and remote Shell buffers are unchanged.

** Reading and navigation fixes

EPUB filenames, downloaded paths and web URLs are passed literally to external
tools, including names containing spaces or shell punctuation. Scanner image
processing preserves the originals if processing fails.

Password prompts retain their speech during minibuffer setup. Incremental
search restores the previous message-speech preference when it ends, and Magit
filenames retain their voices across selection highlights.

Released notes are preserved in [[file:NEWS-2026.9.4][NEWS for Emacsvox 2026.9.4]].
